WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:06.200
So, you now have two very similar problems
for genome assembly, but

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:12.980
it turned out that these two problems have
very different algorithmic fates.

00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:15.870
In the Eulerian path problem, we want to
find

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:20.030
a path visiting every edge of the graph
exactly once.

00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:22.450
In the Hamiltonian path problem, we want
to

00:00:22.450 --> 00:00:26.480
find a path visiting every node exactly
once.

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:29.710
It sounds like small, cosmetic and
unimportant information.

00:00:30.790 --> 00:00:32.400
Let me ask you a question.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:33.990
If you were given the possibility

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:37.320
to choose between the Hamiltonian Path
Problem

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:44.010
and Eulerian Path Problem as presented in
this slide what choice would you make?

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:45.120
Son?

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:48.060
&gt;&gt; Son: Well, I would like to solve the
Eulerian path

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:52.010
problem because the graph on the right
looks simpler, and

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:56.550
maybe it's easier to find a path that
traverses all the edges in the graph.

00:00:56.550 --> 00:01:03.390
&gt;&gt; Son just gave a very wrong answer.

00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:08.130
It doesn't matter that the one graph looks
simpler than another.

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:13.305
In real life, this graph will contain
millions, if not billions of

00:01:13.305 --> 00:01:17.250
edges, and it doesn't matter that one of
them contains 2 million and

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:22.280
another contains 1 million of edges.
What matter is whether we have

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:27.370
efficient algorithms for solving this
problem.

00:01:27.370 --> 00:01:29.940
And it turns out that the different

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:32.320
algorithmic fate of this problem is 
related

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:39.588
to the fact that Euler actually
found an algorithm for efficiently solving

00:01:39.588 --> 00:01:40.322
the Eulerian path problem
26
00:01:40,322 --&gt; 00:01:44,620
Path Problem but a generation of computer
scientists failed

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:48.710
to find an efficient algorithm for
Hamiltonian path problem.

00:01:48.710 --> 00:01:54.055
In fact, the Hamiltonian path problem
belongs to a class of so called

00:01:54.055 --> 00:01:58.720
incomplete problem that contains thousands
of computational

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:03.430
problems for which algorithms are still
unknown.

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:04.920
For some time,

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:08.040
computer scientists tried to find out
whether

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:10.750
this problem can be solved in principle or

00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:13.670
whether maybe there is not

00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:17.460
even an efficient algorithm for solving
this problem.

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:20.770
Maybe even God cannot solve this problem
efficiently.

00:02:20.770 --> 00:02:24.880
But, it turns out to be very difficult to
prove that there

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:30.120
is no efficient algorithm for this problem,
and in fact, the Hamiltonian

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:32.830
path problem belongs to one of

00:02:32.830 --> 00:02:37.610
seven millennium problems of mathematics
that remain unsolved.

00:02:37.610 --> 00:02:42.775
However, what computer scientists were
able to prove is that all

00:02:42.775 --> 00:02:48.770
NP-complete problems, which means thousands
of problem from this class are equivalent.

00:02:48.770 --> 00:02:52.170
Or in other words if you solve any of

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:55.750
these problems you will solve the
Hamiltonian path problem.

00:02:55.750 --> 00:02:58.540
And as a result, our failure to solve the

00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:01.980
Hamiltonian path problem becomes a little
bit less upsetting.

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:05.280
Because we know that we failed to solve it
but we

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:10.290
also know that thousands of computer
scientists also failed to solve it.

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:15.900
And since our chances to solve this
problem are slim, from now on we will

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:21.620
focus on the Eulerian path problem.
And we'll study in more details.

